  "text": "To factor the given expression, we begin by rewriting it in a form that makes a grouping of terms easier:\n\n$$\n3z^4 - 4z^3 + 5z^2 - 11z + 2.\n$$\n\n### Step 1: Grouping the terms\n\nGroup the terms in pairs to see if we can find a pattern:\n\n$$\n(3z^4 - 4z^3) + (5z^2 - 11z + 2).\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Factoring out the GCD from each grouped term\n\nFactor out the greatest common divisor (GCD) from each group:\n\n$$\n3z^3(z - \\frac{4}{3}) + z(5z - 11) + 2.\n$$\n\nThis step is not necessary as we'll be factoring the entire expression. Let's go back and try again.\n\n### Step 3: Factoring out a common factor from the entire expression\n\nWe can factor out the common factor from the entire expression directly:\n\nFor the expression $3z^4 - 4z^3 + 5z^2 - 11z + 2$, we will try to factor it as follows:\n\nAssume it factors into two quadratic expressions:\n\n$$\n3z^4 - 4z^3 + 5z^2 - 11z + 2 = (az^2 + bz + c)(dz^2 + ez + f).\n$$\n\nBy comparing and solving, we find a specific pair of factors that works:\n\n$$(3z^2 - z + 1)(z^2 - 2z + 2).$$\n\n### Step 4: Finding the roots of the quadratic factors\n\nWe set each quadratic factor to zero to find the roots:\n\nFor $(3z^2 - z + 1) = 0$:\n\nUsing the quadratic formula $z = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$, we get:\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4(3)(1)}}{2(3)} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 12}}{6} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{-11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have complex roots:\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{1}{6} \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}i.\n$$\n\nFor $(z^2 - 2z + 2) = 0$:\n\nUsing the quadratic formula again, we get:\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^2 - 4(1)(2)}}{2(1)} = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 - 8}}{2} = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} = \\frac{2 \\pm 2i}{2} = 1 \\pm i.\n$$\n\n### Combine the roots\n\nThe roots of the expression $3z^4 - 4z^3 + 5z^2 - 11z + 2 = 0$ are:\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{1}{6} \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}i, 1 \\pm i.\n$$\n\nThus, the complex roots of the equation $3z^4 - 4z^3 + 5z^2 - 11z + 2 = 0$ are:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6} \\left(1 \\pm \\sqrt{11}i\\right), 1 \\pm i}.\n$$",
